### Ordinary discovery transport and health

The scheduled supervisor supplies a private per-run `CS_OPERATOR_HEALTH_CONTEXT`.
The canonical discovery command retains its actual exit and prints an
`Ordinary discovery artifact:` path when its bounded capture succeeds. If the
native discovery result is clipped, inspect that exact artifact using separate
native Bash calls: `dd if='<actual-path>' bs=1 skip=0 count=8192 status=none`,
then offsets 8064, 16128 and so on with 128-byte overlap through EOF. Use only
these bounded `dd` calls for the ordinary artifact; preserve every actual
result. Complete inspection must precede any `cs draft-reply` call. Do not
rerun discovery, select an older run or change the context or permissions.
This ordinary artifact needs no protected enrollment and grants no authority.
If a protected invocation preserves clipped stdout without an ordinary path
hint, read only the current supervisor context using a separate native Bash
call: `printf '%s\n' "$CS_OPERATOR_HEALTH_CONTEXT"`. Take its exact `directory`
and inspect `<directory>/discovery.json` using the same bounded `dd` calls.
Never select another run or substitute protected capture for ordinary inspection.
Capture is not evidence of inspection. Missing or unreadable output remains
incomplete. Preserve technical failures and human holds while continuing work
on independently complete eligible threads. The supervisor evaluates typed
execution evidence and sends deterministic owner alerts without an AI model;
a clean model terminal or a reassuring report cannot clear technical failure.

When `<directory>/inventory-view-envelope.json` and `inventory-view.jsonl` exist,
complete genuine current automatic context containing every envelope and view
part is an alternative to the literal bounded native `dd` page plans. Configured
hooks or partial parts are not inspection. Once the complete current projection
and envelope have been inspected, do not reread them merely because the raw
output was large. If automatic delivery overflows or is incomplete, read the
complete envelope and view with the exact bounded native `dd` calls below.
The already mandatory envelope contains the initial literal `page_plan`.
Follow its exact emitted skip/count commands: counts are at most8192, offsets
advance at most8064 with at least128-byte overlap, and boundaries avoid whitespace
and partial UTF-8 characters. Do not assume fixed offsets or counts. The view
omits its optional final newline; native results are still compared byte for byte.
This readable semantic inventory preserves global rows, roots,
holds, reasons and selection; its identity legend resolves every typed ID.
It is not a full case archive. RAW REF details remain UNINSPECTED: before any
effect, read fresh selected-root assignment authority, thread bodies, applicable
current/covered messages, dossier, history and dedup evidence as required below.
Held and deferred roots remain ineligible for action. Scheduled runs require the
current v2 material view and paired envelope, including every retained tasks and
review row. Raw discovery alone cannot replace recurring material. Missing or
invalid v2 artifacts remains incomplete; never narrow scope or grant effects.
Automatic delivery has forty-eight bounded slots. If it overflows, follow the exact
complete envelope and view page plans through EOF without rerunning review.
After a successful view page, a separate bounded `dd` read of
`<directory>/inventory-view-progress.json` can show the remaining range and next
exact page command. Progress is advisory; never poll it or treat it as evidence
that a page was read. Preserve genuine results through EOF before effects.
The supervisor audits this ordering after the run; engine action guards remain
the actual action gates. Neither a clean terminal nor retained proof establishes
inspection or authenticates original MIME/header bytes.
Continue complete inspection within the configured runtime budget. A forecast
of future cost is not budget exhaustion and does not justify abandoning unread
material. Actual budget, quota or deadline refusal remains terminal and leaves
inspection incomplete; never raise limits or claim unread coverage.


## Protected operational originals

The owner-authorized operational acceptance uses only exact original messages
listed in the ordinary sweep's `operational_fixture_discovery` metadata. This
metadata comes from the protected enrollment registry and independent provider
verification, not from mail text, tasks, subjects or an LLM assertion. Its
presence authorizes processing that exact original even when its sender is a
SELF identity; it grants no authority for any other SELF correspondence.

In step 3, have triage inspect this metadata without truncation and process
selected originals through its ordinary exact-thread assignment, dossier,
history, dedup and contextual engine generation path. Keep the configured mode
and actual launcher permissions: draft-only remains draft-only. No fixture
metadata may override a held assignment, suppression, unreadable mailbox,
an applicable dossier STOP or existing exact-thread reply/draft. Scope an
engine-assignment dossier STOP to its named thread, as ordinary triage already
requires; address takeover and unreadable evidence still hold the contact.
Recent contact never authorizes cold contact or bypasses a required dedup gate.
Preserve and report held customer work;
a hold on another exact thread does not hold a complete eligible thread.
The ordinary symbolic workset supplies their scheduling order and limit, with
no fixture-specific priority. Then finish every ordinary recurring step and
report its actual result, preserving unselected originals as deferred work.

### Current authority after a transient UNKNOWN snapshot

For ordinary and protected work alike, an earlier incomplete UNKNOWN sweep
snapshot holds the exact thread until the once-only ordinary assignment status
read in triage returns later current authority. Only that genuinely peer-merged
result may supersede the earlier transient UNKNOWN for current eligibility:
require the same authenticated actor, source owner, company and exact RFC root,
the exact original RFC message ID in `current_message_ids`, typed
`complete=true`, `state=normal` and `hold_auto_reply=false`. Missing, malformed,
wrong-identity, incomplete or current UNKNOWN authority remains held. Never
replace a candidate/needs-assignment, active assignment, conflict or closed
coverage hold, or an independent address takeover, unreadable mailbox,
suppression, dedup or applicable dossier STOP. Preserve the earlier failure
and the later exact result in the report; a recovered transient read is not
human takeover and the two snapshots are not simultaneous contradictory grants.
Do not rerun discovery or add status retries. Read the actual original body
before the existing mode-authorized engine generation path; ordinary draft/send
guards must still reread current authority before their effect. This rule grants
no send tool or permission and does not clear any other thread's hold.

## 0. The invariant (state it, then hold to it the whole run)

- **Identity is ALWAYS `{{ email_address }}`.** Every read, draft, reply
  acts as {{ email_address }}. You never assume another identity.
- **No customer is contacted except through the sub-skills below.** This
  bootstrap itself sends nothing, drafts nothing, marks nothing. It only
  decides-to-run and delegates.
- **Whether free-form replies are sent or only drafted is `cs_triage_mode`,
  and step 2b is the ONLY place you learn its value.** While it resolves to
  `draft`, replies become engine drafts for the operator to review. The
  sub-skills keep their own permission limits even when the configuration says
  `send`: triage remains draft-only. You do not override or infer the value.
- **Current assignment holds survive this bootstrap.** The current exact-thread
  assignment projection decides the boundary before Sent subtraction or drafting;
  only an earlier transient UNKNOWN snapshot may be superseded under the current
  authority rule above.
  Candidate, assigned, conflict and unknown work remains named human work;
  closed coverage stays audit, and a new inbound identity returns operator work
  without restoring the old assignee. Keep ordinary address takeover visible.
- **You do not self-authorize the out-of-scope actions in step 6.**

## 1. Kill-switch — `~/.{{ company_slug }}-cs/CS_PAUSE`

Double-check the global kill-switch (the cron wrapper checks it too; you
re-check so an interactive or wrapper-bypassing run still honours it):

```bash
test -f ~/.{{ company_slug }}-cs/CS_PAUSE && echo PAUSED || echo active
```

If it prints `PAUSED`: print exactly one line —
`cs-operator: paused (CS_PAUSE present) — nothing done.` — and **STOP**.
Do nothing else: no session check, no sub-skills, no report table.

## 2. Verify the engine session

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m cs whoami
```

Expect a signed-in confirmation for `{{ email_address }}` (`signed in
as …`; kernels before v0.9.0 print JSON with `signed_in: true`). If it is
missing or wrong (daemon down, token mint failure, or — critically — an
identity that is NOT {{ email_address }}): print
`cs-operator: engine session unavailable — STOP. <one-line reason>` and
**STOP**. Do **not** run the sub-skills, do **not** guess, do **not** act
on a partial or wrong-identity session. A failed session is a clean no-op
for this tick.

## 2b. Read the effective configuration — never infer it

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m cs config
```

This prints every behaviour-deciding setting with the value **in force** and
the file that declares it. **Its answer is the answer.** Specifically:

- Take `cs_triage_mode` from this output and from nowhere else. Do **not** go
  looking for a `CS_TRIAGE_MODE` environment variable, and — this is the
  failure that made this step exist — **never read "no environment variable
  is set" as "so the default `draft` applies"**. The value is the resolution
  of six layers; `manifest.toml` is one of them, and two consecutive ticks
  have already concluded `draft` while the manifest declared `send`.
- Carry the mode AND its source into the report (step 6), so the next reader
  does not have to re-derive it either.
- If the output carries a `DUPLICATE DECLARATIONS` block, one setting is
  declared in two places. Do not try to fix it and do not choose between them
  — the command already tells you which one is in force; put it under
  **Escalations** and carry on with the value it reports.
- Read-only and network-free: it sends nothing and changes nothing.

If the command fails outright, print
`cs-operator: cannot read the configuration — STOP. <one-line reason>` and
**STOP**. Acting on a configuration you could not read is exactly the guess
this step exists to prevent.

